<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Manuel Azaña</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1880">1880</a>–<a href="/years/1940">1940</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Manuel Azaña was a Spanish writer and politician, prime minister of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic</a> in 1931 to 1933 and again in 1936, and its president from 1936 to 1939. His governments pursued army reform, land reform, Catalan autonomy and separation of Church and state. He resigned after the Republic's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a> and died in exile in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/manuel-azana.jpg" alt="Manuel Azaña" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manuel_Aza%C3%B1a_cropped.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Manuel Azaña Díaz was born on 10 January 1880 into a wealthy family and was orphaned young. He took a doctorate at the Universidad Complutense in 1900, worked from 1909 in the Directorate of the Registries and practised as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-law">civil law</a> notary. He joined the Reformist Republican Party in 1914, wrote for newspapers and was secretary of the Ateneo de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> from 1913 to 1920. He published a manifesto against the dictator Primo de Rivera and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alfonso-xiii">Alfonso XIII</a> in 1924, and in 1930 signed the Pact of San Sebastián, which united the republican and regionalist parties.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the Second Republic was proclaimed on 14 April 1931 Azaña became Minister of War and then, in October, prime minister, leading a coalition of left-wing parties and the Socialists. His government expanded public education, passed labour laws, reduced the size of the army and abolished Church-operated schools and charities. An agrarian law, passed late in 1932, was little carried out: only 12,000 families received land in the first two years. Alcalá-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zamora">Zamora</a> ordered his resignation on 8 September 1933, and the right won the election that November.</p><p>Azaña founded the Republican Left in 1934 and helped build the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/popular-front-chile">Popular Front</a>, which won the election of 16 February 1936. He became prime minister again on 19 February and was elected President of the Republic on 7 April 1936. On 17 July a military rising began the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-civil-war">Spanish Civil War</a>. From October 1936 he never lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a> again, and on 5 February 1939 he crossed into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> on foot.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Azaña resigned as president on 3 March 1939 rather than return to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrid">Madrid</a>, a decision that Nationalist and Republican commentators alike condemned as desertion. After the German invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> the Mexican government gave him citizenship and the rank of honorary ambassador, so that he could not be arrested. He died at Montauban on 3 November 1940, and the flag of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a> covered his coffin. His diaries are a main source for the Republican government in the war, and his speech of 18 July 1938 asked for peace, pity and pardon. Piers Brendon calls him a brilliant speaker but a clumsy actor in politics, and he has become a hero of the left in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Manuel Azaña” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Radical and Socialist majority then postponed regular elections for two more years, and the government of <b>Manuel Azaña</b> began numerous reforms in what it saw as a modernisation of the country.</blockquote><a href="/empire/second-spanish-republic">Second Spanish Republic · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Casa natal de Manuel Azaña, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Montauban, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.37&amp;year=1919" aria-label="Casa natal de Manuel Azaña on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Manuel Azaña"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4781 4105h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4781 4105h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=40.48,-3.37&amp;year=1919">What was here in 1919? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
